<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shibtu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">18th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shibtu was the queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>, in the eighteenth century BCE, and a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarim-lim-i">Yarim-Lim I of Yamhad</a>. Her letters in the Mari archives show her running the palace and its administration, reporting on affairs and consulting prophets and omens while the king was on campaign. She is among the best-documented royal women of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">ancient Near East</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Shibtu, whose Amorite name means the Old One, perhaps the Eldest, was born into the royal house of Yamhad at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, one of the most powerful kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> in the early 18th century BCE. Her mother was Queen Gashera, and her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> later succeeded their father. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> had recovered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> with Yarim-Lim's support, and their alliance was sealed by her marriage, which took place by proxy in about 1773 BCE, the second year of his reign: his envoys placed the veil on the bride at Aleppo after long talks over her dowry and the counter-dowry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> already had a main wife, Dam-hurasim, apparently a daughter of the king of Qatna, and Shibtu arrived in a secondary position. After the death of the queen mother Addu-duri she rose to the first rank. She stayed in the palace when the king was away, managing its staff, stores and estates, and her letters often open by saying that the palace is well. Yet she remained subordinate: she reported to him in detail and refused to hand over gold to the steward Mukannishum without a written order from the king.</p><p>She took a large part in religious affairs. She made offerings, among them gifts to the goddess Ishtar of Tuba in her homeland, reported prophecies uttered in the sanctuaries, and at the king's request had omens taken from the entrails of lambs. One such reading wrongly predicted that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> would never prevail over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a>. Zimri-Lim's letters to her also deal with the choice of captive women for his household and with an infection spreading among the palace women, which he told her to contain by keeping the sick woman's cup, seat and bed apart.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Shibtu bore twins, a boy and a girl, probably the Hadni-Addu and Shubultum listed with her in the Small Eastern Palace. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hammurabi">Hammurabi</a>'s troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mari">Mari</a> in 1761 BCE and the palace was looted and destroyed, she and the king disappear from the record. The scholar J.-M. Durand has suggested from a tablet listing her dowry that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimri-lim">Zimri-Lim</a> may have died before the fall, and that Shibtu either fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> with her dowry or was remarried to Dadi-hadun, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> king.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Shibtu” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Royal women held real authority: Zimri-Lim’s queen <b>Shibtu</b>, daughter of Yarim-Lim of Yamhad, managed the palace and reported on affairs while he was on campaign, and his daughters were married to vassal kings in the Khabur region to bind them to Mari.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mari">Mari · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Mari, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.55,40.89&amp;year=-1735" aria-label="Mari on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Shibtu"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5762 4575h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5762 4575h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=34.55,40.89&amp;year=-1735">What was here in 1735 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
